    "uid": "scaling-laws/arXiv%3A2603.15389",
    "title": "When Does Sparsity Mitigate the Curse of Depth in LLMs",
    "citation": "Dilxat Muhtar et al., 2026",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15389",
    "authors": "Dilxat Muhtar; Xin-Yuan Song; Sebastian Pokutta; Max Zimmer; Nico Pelleriti; Thomas Hofmann; Shi-Wei Liu",
    "year": "2026",
    "original_source": false
  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports a controlled depth/parameter sweep with validation perplexity and downstream-task scores, but does not fit or replicate the cited scaling-law methods.",
    "gate_note": "The study's own results include validation perplexity across four model sizes and downstream accuracy across the same depth sweep. Its variance, layer-effectiveness, and sparsity results do not directly test the claim versions and are not rows here. It does not test token- or compute-scaling laws, extrapolation, compute-optimal allocation, or architecture effects on loss at fixed parameter count.",
    "test_reason": "A curse-of-depth and sparsity study. It cites Kaplan and Hoffmann only in related work. Its depth sweep (12–32 layers at fixed width, 900M–2.3B parameters, 10B tokens) characterises layer under-utilisation. It fits no power law and spans under 3x in parameters. Its few accuracy points show only a monotone gain with no predictive fit, so it cannot bear on V1 or V6 as stated.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that variance growth in Pre-LN models drives a curse of depth, where deeper layers become under-utilised. They argue that implicit sparsity (weight decay, long context) and explicit sparsity (GQA, MoE) damp variance and improve layer utilisation. Combining these mechanisms gives a 32-layer 1.2B model a reported 4.6-point accuracy gain.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study investigates sparsity and depth utilization rather than fitting parameter-, token-, or compute-scaling laws or finding a compute-optimal parameter/token split.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The study reports that depth utilization and performance vary with weight decay, sequence length, GQA group size, and MoE configuration; these are outside the scaling-law versions tested here.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Depth sweep at fixed width: PPL falls 13.72 to 13.47 as parameters grow 900M to 2.3B (depth 12 to 32). Downstream accuracy rises slightly. Usefulness score falls from 0.75 to 0.53.",
        "proposed_claim": "Increasing depth at fixed width gives diminishing loss returns due to layer under-utilisation (curse of depth).",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r5",
          "r6",
          "r7",
